#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760} :activity/id #uuid "e1b56295-70ee-55b2-8a65-97f8ae733716" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135084 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "0c3e4762-a85a-5881-81ad-857e0bec5a48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e063a5e6-8819-5814-8c62-dae890a57d71" :step/math-academy-id 26178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a10e6c95-833b-528d-a1f9-411415a6a652" :step/math-academy-id 32622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133695 :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "f869fbb2-2435-5488-b530-01a592efdf62" :step/math-academy-id 32627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133706 :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"} :step/id #uuid "879e7253-5b1c-559d-8414-14057286be07" :step/math-academy-id 35588 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135127 :tutorial/content "Let's apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y) = x^2y^3$ and $C$ is the path along the curve $y=x^2$ for $x \\in [0,2].$ \n\nFirst, we must parameterize the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t^2\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of $C$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0)\t= \\left\\langle 0, 0\\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{r}(2)\t= \\left\\langle 2, 4\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the path's endpoints defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,4)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice how much faster it is to evaluate a line integral when we can use the fundamental theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "8ce39e74-c98d-54af-8d93-20f6b5bca39a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5829 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "15f14408-48b8-5f0f-8c55-d5009dc56e57" :step/math-academy-id 35721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135128 :tutorial/content "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ef0926c-7a69-5fc8-91f0-7f40e3be9be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5830 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Fundamental Theorem for Smooth Curves"} :step/id #uuid "48691aa9-a37a-52cd-a44c-e9ef2b10ea85" :step/math-academy-id 35722}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133996}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423278} :activity/id #uuid "f437e194-169c-50f2-945d-004eb6df919b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238492} {:answer/id #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238616} {:answer/id #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239384} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239575} {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "47da3408-dc6c-5c1f-9665-3466da3da017" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} {:answer/id #uuid "8020ab34-3c45-5159-8e7f-6c5ce658702c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ca4d2-0f0c-5594-9b9e-6e91ba3983b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258687} {:answer/id #uuid "e253a7c1-631c-5b5e-ba82-916317b443b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c80ca6-5207-5a6c-a174-ad7e192a275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5dece436-1610-5dc1-95d0-9fdd97342268"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84d1b7ff-6c49-52fd-9e1e-1f9250c2fd44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3aba54e-fa79-516c-9cb2-e92b39bb4228" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} {:answer/id #uuid "32a24c80-640c-5f96-9d30-3886433c358a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe846b-c77b-5114-acc8-ae328a43149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} {:answer/id #uuid "de2288dd-737a-5b6e-99cf-5d7174075a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ff9e85-14c9-5103-9111-1d260075e876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d90c6fd6-ba8c-5bad-bd78-dc112ba8ab5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423277}}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423275}]}
